        imageBg: "linear-gradient(135deg, #2b1055, #7597de)",
        content: `
            <p>Choosing an engineering branch after the 12th grade is one of the most critical decisions a student will ever make. It's the foundation of your career and determines the next four years of your academic life and the rest of your professional journey.</p>
            
            <p>With so many options available—from traditional branches like Civil and Mechanical to modern ones like AI & ML—how do you pick the one that's right for you? In this guide, we'll break down the top engineering branches in VTU and help you make an informed decision.</p>

            <h2>1. Computer Science & Engineering (CSE)</h2>
            <p>CSE remains the most popular and competitive branch. It focuses on the basics of computer programming and networking. The curriculum includes subjects like data structures, algorithms, database management systems, and operating systems.</p>
            <p><strong>Scope:</strong> Huge demand in software development, cybersecurity, cloud computing, and IT services. Companies like Google, Amazon, and Microsoft primarily hire CSE graduates.</p>

            <h2>2. Electronics & Communication Engineering (ECE)</h2>
            <p>ECE is a versatile branch that bridges the gap between hardware and software. You'll study electronic circuits, communication protocols, signal processing, and VLSI design.</p>
            <p><strong>Scope:</strong> Excellent opportunities in telecommunications, robotics, embedded systems, and the semiconductor industry (Intel, Qualcomm, etc.).</p>

            <h2>3. Artificial Intelligence & Machine Learning (AI & ML)</h2>
            <p>A specialized branch that focuses on teaching computers how to learn and make decisions. This branch is part of the "Next-Gen" curriculum and is becoming increasingly popular due to the rise of automation.</p>
            <p><strong>Scope:</strong> High demand in data science, predictive analytics, and building intelligent systems. Companies are paying premium salaries for AI/ML specialists.</p>

            <h2>4. Mechanical Engineering</h2>
            <p>One of the oldest and broadest branches. It involves the design, manufacturing, and maintenance of machines. You'll study thermodynamics, fluid mechanics, machine design, and robotics.</p>
            <p><strong>Scope:</strong> Opportunities in automotive industries, aerospace, energy production, and manufacturing plants.</p>

            <h2>5. Civil Engineering</h2>
            <p>Civil engineering is about the physical and naturally built environment. This includes roads, bridges, canals, and buildings. It's a "classic" branch with significant government job opportunities.</p>
            <p><strong>Scope:</strong> Essential for infrastructure development. High demand in construction companies and government departments like PWD and National Highway Authority.</p>

            <h2>Conclusion</h2>
            <p>Choosing an engineering branch should be a fine balance between your personal interests, your natural aptitude, and current market demand. Don't rush into a branch just because of peer pressure. Research thoroughly, take your time, and remember: regardless of the branch you pick, hard work and a passion for engineering will ultimately define your success.</p>
        `
